window.docContents[255].resolve({"/#eval-io":{"contents":"Lean's  command can be used to execute  actions, rather than just evaluating them.\nNormally, adding a  command to a Lean file causes Lean to evaluate the provided expression, convert the resulting value to a string, and provide that string as a tooltip and in the info window.\nRather than failing because  actions can't be converted to strings,  executes them, carrying out their side effects.\nIf the result of execution is the  value , then no result string is shown, but if it is a type that can be converted to a string, then Lean displays the resulting value.\n\nThis means that, given the prior definitions of  and ,displays\n\nThis is the output produced by running the  action, rather than some opaque representation of the action itself.\nIn other words, for  actions,  both evaluates the provided expression and executes the resulting action value.\n\nQuickly testing  actions with  can be much more convenient that compiling and running whole programs.\nHowever, there are some limitations.\nFor instance, reading from standard input simply returns empty input.\nAdditionally, the  action is re-executed whenever Lean needs to update the diagnostic information that it provides to users, and this can happen at unpredictable times.\nAn action that reads and writes files, for instance, may do so unexpectedly.\n\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Hello, World!\u0009Additional Conveniences","header":"2.5.3. Running  IO  Actions With  #eval","id":"/#eval-io"},"/#step-by-step":{"contents":"A  block can be executed one line at a time.\nStart with the program from the prior section:\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Hello, World!","header":"2.2. Step By Step","id":"/#step-by-step"}});